<title>USA: U.S. April housing starts rose 2.6 percent.</title>
<headline>U.S. April housing starts rose 2.6 percent.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-05-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Commerce housing starts and</p>
<p>building permits, seasonally adjusted annual rates, in 1,000s:</p>
<p> PCT CHANGE: Apr     Mar (Prev)     Feb (Prev)   Apr97/96</p>
<p> -Starts     2.6    -7.7   -6.4    13.0   10.7	 -3.2</p>
<p> -Permits   -0.8     1.3   -1.5     3.1    3.1	 -2.7</p>
<p> RATES	 Apr     Mar (Prev)     Feb (Prev)   Apr 1996</p>
<p> -Starts   1,473   1,435  1,425   1,554  1,522	1,522</p>
<p> -Permits  1,446   1,457  1,423   1,438  1,444	1,486</p>
<p> STARTS:     Apr     Mar (Prev)     Feb (Prev)   Apr 1996</p>
<p> -Single   1,118   1,115  1,117   1,237  1,218	1,215</p>
<p> -Multiple   355     320    308     317    304	  307</p>
<p> PERMITS     Apr     Mar (Prev)     Feb (Prev)   Apr 1996</p>
<p> -Single   1,064   1,034  1,015   1,069  1,077	1,128</p>
<p> -Multiple   382     423    408     369    367	  358</p>
<p> APRIL STARTS--Pct--Rate   APRIL PERMITS-Pct---Rate</p>
<p> -NorthEast   -7.4   137    -Northeast -14.4   131</p>
<p> -Midwest	0.7   292    -Midwest     2.6   315</p>
<p> -South	 11.3   699    -South	 0.3   654</p>
<p> -West	  -6.5   345    -West	  0.3   346</p>
<p>Actual Starts and Permits, unadjusted in 1,000s:</p>
<p>    -	  Apr   Mar   Feb  Apr96</p>
<p>  Starts     141   116    95   147</p>
<p>  Permits    137   120    93   140</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast April starts at a 1.43 million unit annual rate.</p>
<p>April starts of single-family homes were rose 0.3 percent from March, and multiple-unit starts rose 10.9 percent. April permits for single-family units rose 2.9 percent, while permits for multiple unit dwelling fell 9.7 percent.  </p>
<p>The 699,000 unit rate for April housing starts in the South was the highest for that region since July 1995 when it was 728,000 units, the department said.</p>
<p>The 131,000 unit rate for April building permits in the Northeast was at its lowest level since February 1996 when it was at 118,000 units, the department said.</p>
